    "chunk_index": 184,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "04f5124a631e546c02340d24779c8e96",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Another complaint I get and the severe criticism that comes now, Hon. Prime Minister, is - the Ranil Wickremesinghe Government, in order to get funding from the Asian Development Bank, went through a process of unilaterally creating reforms, which was heavily opposed, including by the JVP and yourself; we opposed it as well - that it is more or less the same reforms that are being pushed by this Government. The question is, just for the sake of getting funds of the ADB, are we going to push for those reforms, which were made unilaterally and without any serious consultation with the stakeholders, through your Government? I do not think you should take that blame; I think you should look into that.\n\nHon. Prime Minister, with the school time being extended - I want to mention this - particularly the\n\nThen, Hon. Prime Minister, I would like to take up the matter of teachers serving in difficult areas. If you take Jaffna, the difficult schools have been revised; those have been revised on certain criteria, which, in a place like Jaffna, make no sense. Sixteen schools in the islands in Jaffna have been taken off this list of difficult areas simply because those areas have, for example, a post office, a hospital or something to that effect. But if you take those islands, Hon. Prime Minister, transportation is the biggest problem there. Having a hospital, a bank or a post office being the reason for those schools to be taken off that list of difficult areas makes no sense because the teachers who come from the mainland face that same problem of transportation. So, again, taking 16 schools off that list, I think, does not make sense; it just does not make sense. I can go through a whole list of schools in the Northern Province and the Eastern Province which have the same problem. Therefore, that criteria is just irrelevant for our areas."
  },
