    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/22979_english_2025-11-25.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-11-25-2025-11-25-0209",
    "chunk_index": 209,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8d50f962c1c7a11dfcebd238f89cc65c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, I acknowledge the progress in introducing smart classrooms, digitized textbooks and online learning platforms. However, the digital divide still affects thousands of students, especially in rural districts. Strengthening ICT education, computer labs, AI literacy and mathematics instruction is essential. No child must be left behind simply because they lack a device or Internet access.\n\n1885 1886\n\n\u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db4\u0dda\u0dca \u0dd4\u0db8\u0dc0\n\nMany schools, particularly in the Eastern Province, still lack science labs, libraries, IT facilities and even basic sanitation facilities. Therefore, I urge the Hon. Prime Minister to prioritize infrastructure investments so that the learning environment of a child in the Eastern Province is equal to that of a child anywhere else in the country. Education reform must begin by ensuring equal facilities for all.\n\nHon. Prime Minister, the job market is changing rapidly. Therefore, our education reforms must focus on equipping our youth with practical, employable skills. Strengthening Technical Colleges, NVQ pathways, apprenticeships and industry-linked training are essential. Sectors such as renewable energy, agriculture technology, maritime, health sciences and ICT would offer great opportunities for our youth, if we educate them properly.\n\nOur education system must serve all communities equally. Strengthening our universities is essential for national development. We must expand university capacity, promote research and innovation and build a strong university-industry partnership. Supporting collaborations with international campuses would also help retain our talent within Sri Lanka.\n\nEducation reform is not a luxury; it is a national necessity. If we modernize the curriculum, empower teachers, digitize learning, strengthen infrastructure and provide equal opportunities for all communities, we can create a generation capable of leading Sri Lanka towards progress and prosperity."
  },
