    "chunk_size_bytes": 2034,
    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dab\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0dd9\u0dc4\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bb0\u0bb5\u0bbf \u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0ba3\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95) (The Hon. Ravi Karunanayake) Hon. Speaker, I wish to ask the following Question under Standing Order No. 27(2) from the Hon. Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development on the subject of the establishment of an independent Employees' Provident Fund Authority. Under the new rule reducing the time allotted for this, I presume I would get the Answer today itself.\n\nOver 90 per cent of EPF assets remain concentrated in Government securities, yielding sub-optimal returns, while equity holdings in regulated banks raise concerns of regulatory imbalance. International practice shows that provident funds are best managed by autonomous authorities such as EPFO in India, KWSP in Malaysia, CPF in Singapore and NPS in Korea, ensuring transparency, diversification and stronger returns that do not have conflicts of interest.\n\nSir, I wish to ask the following questions from the Hon. Minister. Will the Hon. Minister state,\n\n1. whether the Government recognizes a conflict of interest in CBSL\u2019s dual role under Act No. 16 of 2023, which means that it is a regulator and an operator;\n\n2. whether steps will be taken to establish an independent EPF Authority with its own governance, professional management and Parliamentary oversight;\n\n3. the current total asset value and investment composition of the EPF and whether concentration in Government securities has reduced contributors\u2019 real returns;\n\n2203 2204\n\n2025 \u0d9a\u0db1\u0ddc\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb 27\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s EPF gets about 30 per\n\ncent while the EPF that the normal people, you and we, contribute gets only 11 per cent.\n\n4. why, despite IMF recommendations - this was one of their conditions under the Debt Sustainability Agreement as well as in their management review to the Government - debt management was removed from CBSL to avoid policy conflicts, but the EPF was retained and whether this inconsistency will be rectified;"
  },
